return await window.__createIslands([{"id":"ess-36","kind":"prose","x":6450,"y":300,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>Matter is often granted memory. It bears marks, scars, inscriptions, residues, ruins, sediments, and archives. Energy is more often imagined as passing: a flash, a pulse, a transmission, a charge, an event that leaves only secondary effects. Yet this division is philosophically insufficient. Energetic events alter systems. Radiation persists through decay. Electromagnetic pulses can leave residual differences. Fields can be modified. A wave can pass and still leave the world otherwise. To say that energy remembers is not to anthropomorphise energy, nor to claim that it remembers as minds or archives do. It is to insist that memory must be thought beyond the familiar forms of inscription on stable substrates. There are differences that continue, traces that are field-like rather than objectual, afterlives that cannot be reduced to documentation. This is the speculative horizon of the exposition: not the invisible as absence, not the imperceptible as lack, not energy as mere carrier, but energy as participant in the persistence of events. Such a horizon changes how artworks involving radiation, transmission, lightning, projection, simulation, and radio propagation may be understood. They do not simply pass through the world. They may leave it minutely, measurably, conceptually, or irreversibly altered.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-7","kind":"prose","x":4600,"y":100,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>On the Plains of San Agustín, twenty-seven dish antennas — each twenty-five metres across — sit on tracks that let them be reconfigured from compact to thirty-nine kilometres apart. Each dish receives radio frequency from space. The signals from all dishes are then combined through a process of interference: the image is not captured in a single optical instant but synthesised from signals gathered across a maximum baseline of thirty-nine kilometres and correlated through computation. Baseline spacing is the instrument. The resolution of the VLA is determined not by the diameter of any individual dish but by the distance between them. The array sees by separating. What no single element could discern, the distributed system synthesises from the differential arrival of the same wavefront at different points in space. Earth is not incidental to this apparatus. As the planet rotates, the geometry of the array shifts, the effective aperture grows, and the planet itself becomes a component of the synthetic lens. The VLA does not magnify; it correlates. Its resolution is a function of separation, not accumulation.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-8","kind":"prose","x":900,"y":1200,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>A grid of polished steel rods in the New Mexico desert can be seen as sculpture, drawing, architecture, meteorological instrument, or philosophical proposition. Its rods stand in measured intervals, their tips aligned to produce a horizontal plane across uneven land. In ordinary conditions, they catch and reflect light; at certain times they almost disappear; under storm conditions they become charged with the possibility of conducting lightning. The work is famous for an event that most visitors will not see. This is essential. Its aesthetic power is not reducible to the spectacle of a strike, but to the durational arrangement of expectation: the waiting, the horizon, the knowledge of possible discharge, the conversion of landscape into field. The work stages reception as potential. It allows the viewer to inhabit a relation between geometric precision and atmospheric contingency. Its rods do not simply occupy the desert; they tune it. They mark the land as a place where electricity may pass, where sky and ground might briefly enter visible violence, where sculpture becomes less a fixed object than a prepared relation to an energetic event. Lightning need not appear for the work to operate. The poles are already grounded; the circuit is already closed. What lightning adds is not reception but visibility of what was always occurring. The possibility of reception is already part of reception.</p></div>"}]);